{"qid":"Q104028","names":["Judea"],"title":"Judea","endonym":null,"capsule":"Judea (Greek: Ἰουδαία; Hebrew: יהודה) as an independent or semi-independent polity spans from the formal establishment of Hasmonean sovereignty under Simon Maccabaeus in 141 BCE — after the Maccabean revolt expelled the Seleucid garrison from Jerusalem — through its transformation into a Roman province in 6 CE. Pompey's conquest of 63 BCE reduced the kingdom to a Roman client state; Herod the Great ruled 37–4 BCE as Rome's client king, rebuilding the Temple on a vast scale and founding Caesarea Maritima, but his death triggered dynastic conflict and Rome deposed his son Archelaus in 6 CE, converting Judea into a Roman prefecture. The region's persistent tension between Jewish monotheism and Roman imperial expectations fueled the Great Revolt of 66–73 CE, culminating in the destruction of the Second Temple and the garrisoning of Legion X Fretensis in Jerusalem.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The core territory of Judea — the southern hill country around Jerusalem — is well-attested, but its precise borders varied substantially across the Hasmonean, Herodian, and Roman periods covered by the attestation window.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Livius.org: Judaea","url":"https://www.livius.org/articles/place/judaea/"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Judea","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Judea"}],"britannica":"place/Judaea","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Judea","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Simon Maccabeus","nativeName":"שמעון החשמונאי","roman":"Shimon HaChashmonai","start":-142,"end":-134,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":n